AB  - This study aims to examine tea flower arrangements (dahwa) suitable for winter by utilizing the Twenty-Four Solar Terms. Focusing on the six winter terms—Ipdong (Beginning of Winter), Soseol (Minor Snow), Daeseol (Major Snow), Dongji (Winter Solstice), Sohan (Minor Cold), and Daehan (Major Cold)—this research develops its argument through their expression in tea flower arrangement works. The formal structure of the works adopts the principles of traditional Oriental flower arrangement. First, winter within the framework of the Twenty-Four Solar Terms is systematically reviewed. Then, based on the records in Yanghwa Soryok by Kang Hee-an, which document sixteen types of flowering plants and ornamental rocks—including pine, juniper, bamboo, chrysanthemum, plum blossom, orchid, daphne, lotus, pomegranate flower, gardenia, seasonal flowers, camellia, crape myrtle, azalea, and citrus—this study explores the floral culture of past scholars. Furthermore, it examines the aesthetic significance of Oriental-style tea flower arrangements characterized by the beauty of line and empty space. Using winter-appropriate materials such as camellia, pine, bamboo, Korean nut pine, dwarf bamboo, cypress, yew, rowan, citrus tree, pussy willow, orchid, and ornamental stones, a total of twelve works were created and structured.
