AB  - The civil construction industry generates high environmental impacts, making sustainable concrete essential to reverse this scenario. This study applied Life Cycle Assessment in Viçosa (MG) to analyze four typologies: recycled aggregates, ornamental stone waste, a 70% replacement of cement with fly ash (CV70), and the addition of red mud. Through the Potential Eco-efficiency Index (EEP), the CV70 mixture stood out, as its pozzolanic effect maintains strength while ensuring reduced environmental impacts, presenting a maximum Greenhouse Gas Reduction (RGE) index. Recycled aggregates and stone waste concretes were also regionally competitive, ranking second in performance. Red mud obtained a lower EEP due to logistical and energy bottlenecks. It is concluded that fly ash and recycled aggregates provide the best balance between sustainability and local technical viability, reducing resource consumption.
