TI  - Aplicação sequencial de wetlands construídos e adsorção em materiais alternativos para otimizar a remoção de fósforo em efluentes domésticos
AU  - Rosa, Mateus da
PY  - 2026
JO  - UFSM
UR  - http://repositorio.ufsm.br/handle/1/38484
AB  - Phosphorus removal from domestic wastewater is essential to maintain the quality of the receiving water body, preventing potential imbalances caused by excess nutrients in the aquatic ecosystem.Constructed wetlands are a highly efficient technology for domestic wastewater treatment; however, they present limitations regarding phosphorus removal. Therefore, the objective of this study was to evaluate the efficiency of constructed wetlands in phosphorus removal and to implement a final polishing step through adsorption columns filled with alternative materials. Marble waste, amethyst mining waste, and water treatment plant sludge (WTS) were tested in laboratory assays for phosphorus adsorption from domestic wastewater. The effects of calcination, different contact times, and pH variation on adsorption efficiency were also evaluated. Marble and amethyst wastes did not show satisfactory laboratory results and were therefore discarded. Water treatment sludge achieved an average removal of 50%, increasing to 80% after calcination.Twelve PVC columns, each 1 meter long, were assembled: three filled with powdered WTS, three with 2 mm WTS, three with 4.75 mm WTS, and three with powdered activated carbon. At the beginning of operation, the columns filled with powdered material clogged and were discontinued. Qualitative analysis of the effluent included color, turbidity, pH, dissolved oxygen (DO), temperature, and total phosphorus (P-total). The wetlands achieved removals of 54% and 63% in units filled with sand at saturation heights of 0.15 m and 0.25 m, respectively. For units filled with gravel, removals were 32% and 36% at the same saturation heights. Plant uptake accounted for less than 1% of total phosphorus removal. The columns showed good performance in color and turbidity removal, while pH, temperature, and DO did not show significant changes after passing through the columns. Total phosphorus removal in the columns was approximately 30%, with no significant difference among the tested particle sizes. Considering the high effluent pH (around 9) and based on laboratory results, the effluent was acidified prior to adsorption, increasing the average phosphorus removal to approximately 50%. The results demonstrate that although constructed wetlands present limitations in total phosphorus removal, their efficiency can be significantly enhanced through a final polishing step using adsorption columns. The identification of water treatment sludge as a promising alternative material, especially after calcination and pH adjustment, highlights the potential for waste reuse as a technically viable and environmentally sustainable solution, promoting circular economy principles and adding value to water treatment by-products. Furthermore, this study provides experimental data applicable to domestic wastewater treatment, supporting the improvement of decentralized systems and expanding alternatives for phosphorus control.
