AB  - In dimensional stone quarries squared blocks are normally extracted using cutting technologies according to regular bench methods. However if the rock is intensely fractured so that unshaped blocks already exist, delimited by sets of natural discontinuities, the use of explosive as the preliminary phase for their selective extraction from the quarry face can be the only option available. These blocks are then towed to the finishing area where final shaping and sizing is carried out. It is clear that explosive must be used very carefully on order that minor damage is induced to the stone and no loss in recovery is incurred. To this end the behaviour of the rock mass under the action of the blast can be simulated by means of numerical codes capable of taking into account the main families of discontinuities and their spatial orientation. In this way the effect of the different parameters (direction and spacing of blast-holes, explosive type and load, blasting sequence, height and width of the bench) can be put into light enabling to suggest the most appropriate blasting design aimed at improving the technical outcome and the economic profitability of the quarry.
