AB  - This chapter presents a life cycle assessment (LCA) of concrete modified with quarry-derived powders, granite (GP), limestone (LP), and quartz (QP), as partial replacements for Portland cement. The study aims to quantify the environmental benefits of utilising quarry wastes in cementitious composites, focusing on reductions in carbon emissions and energy demand. Conducted in accordance with ISO 14040 and ISO 14044 standards, the LCA follows a cradle-to-gate approach and incorporates experimentally validated concrete formulations from previous chapters. To improve the interpretability and comparability of results, four strength-normalised indicators are introduced: the Emission Indicator ( IEM ), Cement Indicator ( IC ), Waste Reuse Indicator ( IW ), and Energy Demand Indicator ( IEN ). These metrics allow for the assessment of both environmental impact and mechanical performance across diverse mix designs. Results indicate that GP and QP offer notable environmental advantages, particularly at 28 and 90 days of curing, while LP is less effective due to its limited contribution to compressive strength. The study demonstrates that performance-based indicators offer a more meaningful evaluation of sustainable concrete than conventional mass-based metrics. The findings support the strategic use of quarry waste in concrete mix design and highlight its potential to contribute to carbon-reduction targets and circular economy objectives in the construction sector.
