AB  - Self-Compacting Concrete (SCC) is a highly flowable, non-segregating concrete that spreads readily into place, fills formwork, and encapsulates reinforcement without any need for mechanical vibration. Its adoption improves construction efficiency, reduces labour and noise, and enhances the durability of the finished structure. In parallel, the rapid growth of the construction sector has led to an ever-increasing generation of Construction and Demolition (C&D) waste, most of which is still landfilled, causing resource depletion and environmental degradation. Utilising C&D waste as a partial replacement for natural fine aggregate in SCC therefore offers a dual benefit: sustainable waste management and conservation of natural resources. This study reports an experimental investigation into the fresh and hardened properties of SCC in which 10% of the natural fine aggregate was replaced with fine aggregate recovered from C&D waste. The mix was proportioned for M20 grade concrete following IS 10262:2019 guidelines, and its fresh-state behaviour was characterised through slump flow, V-funnel, U-box and L-box tests, while its hardened-state behaviour was evaluated through compressive strength testing at 7, 14 and 28 days. The results were benchmarked against normal vibrated concrete with and without C&D waste, and against SCC without waste. The SCC mixes satisfied the EFNARC workability limits for filling ability, passing ability and segregation resistance, and the SCC with C&D waste fine aggregate attained about 99% of the 28-day strength of conventional SCC without waste, confirming that a 10% replacement level is a technically viable and environmentally beneficial option.
