AB  - The development of multifunctional mineral composites combining fire resistance with optical–infrared radiation shielding is of growing importance in sustainable photonic engineering. In this study, phosphorite-based fire-resistant composites were engineered from indigenous mineral resources including low-grade phosphorite, vermiculite, perlite, waste marble, and clay soil. Comprehensive physicochemical characterization was performed using X-ray diffraction (XRD), Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR), thermal analysis (TGA/DTA), scanning electron microscopy (SEM/EDS), and energy-dispersive X-ray fluorescence (EDXRF). Alkaline treatment increased the P₂O₅ content and reduced hygroscopicity of phosphorite samples. The layered silicate structure of vermiculite, high silica content of perlite and clay soil, and CaO-rich phases of marble waste contribute to thermal stability and potential infrared radiation scattering. The microstructural and compositional features indicate promising optical–thermal regulation properties, making these composites suitable for fire-resistant and spectrally selective engineering applications. The results support further investigation of phosphorite-derived materials for photonic heat-management systems and sustainable radiation-shielding technologies.
