PY  - 2026
JO  - Journal of Management & Social Science
DO  - 10.5281/zenodo.21445350
UR  - https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.21445350
AB  - Today's hospitality industry is more dependent than ever on the use of technology, interior design and knowledge-based practices to make memorable and personalised guest experiences. This study aims to explore the effect of digital systems and natural stone interior design on guest experience, and to explore the mediating role of knowledge management and customer behaviour in the hospitality industry. The research method employed was quantitative research with a structured online questionnaire that was distributed to 200 guests who had previously stayed in mid- to high-end hotels. The collected data were analysed using the Partial Least Squares Structural Equation Modelling (PLS-SEM) with the software SmartPLS to assess the measurement and structural model. The results show that digital systems can greatly improve knowledge management, and natural stone interior design can positively affect the guest experience by creating a better aesthetic, environmental comfort, and cultural authenticity. Knowledge management was identified to have a positive impact on customer behaviour, and customer behaviour in turn positively impacted on guest experience by way of positive behavioural intentions and emotional responses. The measurement model was found to have satisfactory reliability and validity, and the structural model was found to support all the proposed hypotheses, which showed that the proposed framework had a moderate predictive power. The research underscores the need to combine the use of sophisticated digital technologies with carefully planned natural stone interiors to facilitate effective knowledge management and improve customer experiences. The results have practical implications for hospitality managers, interior designers, and policy makers, as they show the potential of technology-enabled knowledge management and sustainable interior design to enhance guest satisfaction, perceived service quality, and customer loyalty. The study adds to the existing hospitality research by providing an interdisciplinary approach that integrates the concepts of digital transformation, interior design, knowledge management, and customer behaviour and guest experience in a single empirical model.
