AB  - Marble industries, which are economic backbones, often impose very high cost to the environment and public health. The present study advocates a detailed multidisciplinary assessment of industrial pollution due to processing and manufacturing of marble in the city of Kishangarh, Rajasthan, popularly known as ‘Marble City of India’. The study was carried out to evaluate the spatial and environmental relationships among marble industrial waste generation, multi-compartment environmental degradation, and potential industrial and community health implications in Kishangarh, using an integrated environmental assessment framework. The methodology included extensive air quality monitoring, physicochemical characterization of agricultural soil and groundwater, geospatial analysis of Land use Land Cover (LULC) changes from 2017 to 2022 within a 15 km buffer of the key Marble Waste Dumping Yard (MWDY), and a community-based health survey (n=95). The average concentrations of atmospheric particulate matter (PM₂.₅) was measured at 465.3 µg/m³ in core zone (Zone-A), which is almost 8 times higher than the national standards and 20-50 times higher than WHO limits. XRD analysis of marble waste revealed a distinct pollutant fingerprint of lead (Pb) silicate (4.6%) and copper (Cu) sulphate (1.1%) mineral phases. The spatial contamination gradient was clear, with the highest soil alkalinity (pH=8.26; p<0.05), salinity (EC = 1030.33 µS/cm; p<0.05) and heavy metal concentrations (Cu=2.24 mg/L; p<0.05) being observed in the area closest to the MWDY. The total dissolved solids (3573.33 ppm; p<0.05) and total hardness (1010 mg/L; p<0.05) were extreme. The health survey of the industrial workers and residents revealed a significant concern for occupational exposure. The findings suggest that marble dust exposure may be associated with environmental degradation and self-reported health symptoms, underscoring the importance of environmental monitoring, regulatory oversight, and public health interventions.
