UR  - https://doi.org/10.1201/9781003763505-74
AB  - Building materials, such as dimension stone, concrete and concrete aggregate, brick, and mortar blocks are exposed to solutions containing a variety of cations. The deterioration caused by cations is due both to physical forces, such as osmosis, and due to chemical reaction of the ions with pore walls. Na and K cations, due to their high charge/ionic volume ratios, a adsorbed by the negatively charged surfaces, and attract polar water molecules. In small pore nearly all water is ionicaly held, and creates a high osmotic potential that causes an expansio of the rock. Alkali reactivity of certain siliceous rock particles within concrete is also due to the affinity of the particle surfaces for the Na and K cation. Under conditions of high alkalinity, the cations promote the dissolution of silica and the formation of silica gel. All rocks with small pores (<5qm), large internal surface area, and active, charged surfaces are at risk to cation attack. Large, highly electronegative anions, such as phosphate, may satisfy the ionic charges of the cations, and reduce the osmotic potential. In case of alkali reactivity, the phosphate ions compete with the hydroxyl ions, reducing silica solubility and the gel formation. Treating the porous material with phosphate solution has significantly reduced both the freeze-thaw damage a the alkali reactivity of concrete and concrete aggregate. A patent, covering the method and th chemicals used in the treatment, has been granted.
