AB  - Abstract Using the vortex system, aluminum-based metal matrix composites with 53μm granite dust particles were successfully created in the study. Al-Si alloy served as the matrix material for this investigation. Granite powder, which contains an enormous amount of Al2O3, is the main waste product that is produced when granite stones are chopped. It is widely acknowledged that reinforcement gives MMCs their resistance to wear, and that adding a hard intermetallic compound to the aluminum matrix greatly enhances the wear characteristics. Usually pricey, the reinforcing elements are SiC, Al2O3, etc. The goal of the current research project is to investigate the usage of granite powder as a cheap reinforcing material. This is because granite is widely readily available and has all of these reinforcing components. This was made possible by using the standard stir casting procedure for building the samples. To increase mechanical properties of the samples and learn more about how particle reinforcement influenced the microstructure behavior of the composite, the surface of the samples were examined using an optical microscope. Granite particles mixed within an aluminum matrix enhance the composite’s mechanical properties and matrix material’s hardness. Solid Works, a 3D modeling program, is used to create and model a two-wheeler’s piston. Finite element analysis is performed on the Piston.
