AB  - The rapid growth of the marble industry has generated large amounts of marble slurry waste, causing environmental concerns in developing countries. This study explores the use of marble slurry as a partial replacement material in tuff tile manufacturing, applying a multi-objective optimization framework based on the Design of Experiments (DOE) method. A factorial experimental design tested the effects of varying marble slurry content (0–25%), water–cement ratio, and compaction pressure on key performance indicators such as compressive strength, water absorption, density, and abrasion resistance. Results showed that adding marble slurry significantly affects the physical and mechanical properties of tuff tiles. An optimal replacement ranges of 10–15% was found, where compressive strength increased by up to 16%, and water absorption decreased by about 11%, improving tile durability. Higher slurry content reduced performance due to weakened cement bonding. Statistical analysis (ANOVA) identified marble slurry content as the dominant factor, accounting for over 48% of strength variation. Response Surface Methodology (RSM) helped develop predictive models and optimize process conditions, achieving a peak compressive strength of 30.2 MPa and water absorption of 12.0%. Besides performance gains, the study highlights environmental and economic benefits: waste reduction by 25%, CO₂ emission reduction by 20%, and material cost savings by 15%. This approach offers a sustainable solution for waste management and promotes resource-efficient construction materials within a circular economy framework.
