AB  - This research adopts as a case study an analysis of the evolution of light pollution in Cachoeiro de Itapemirim, Espírito Santo, from 2012 to 2025. The study sought to investigate the relationship between the increase in nighttime radiance and changes in land use and land cover, using remote sensing data (VIIRS sensor) and spatial analysis tools. The results indicate an increase of approximately 29.5% in average radiance, even with a slight decrease in population, showing that light pollution is not directly related to the number of inhabitants. This increase is not uniform and is concentrated mainly in industrial hubs linked to the marble and granite sector, new commercial areas, and recent residential developments. The study highlights the importance of integrating artificial lighting control guidelines into urban planning.
