AB  - The escalating global demand for sustainable infrastructure, coupled with the rapid depletion of natural coarse aggregates, has necessitated the exploration of rapidly renewable, bio-based alternatives within the concrete industry. Concrete plays a pivotal role in civil engineering construction, with demand continuously rising, yet the extraction of natural stone leads to ecological imbalances and depletion of natural resources. This comprehensive paper evaluates the structural, physical, and rheological viability of integrating hardened bamboo chips as a partial volumetric replacement for conventional coarse aggregates in concrete. Operating on a standardized 1:2:4 nominal mix proportion with a water-to-cement ratio of 0.50, the empirical investigation systematically substituted crushed stone with treated bamboo aggregate at replacement thresholds of 0%, 10%, 20%, and 30%. The primary focus of this study centers on extracting and analyzing the resulting density dynamics, workability (slump), and compressive strength over curing periods of 7, 14, and 28 days. The experimental data reveals a decisive inverse correlation between bamboo inclusion and the ultimate bulk density of the hardened concrete, which reduced from 2540 kg/m³ in the control mix to 2437 kg/m³ at the 30% replacement level. Concurrently, workability vastly improved, with slump values increasing from 3 cm at 0% to 13 cm at 30% replacement. While compressive strength decreased from 15.12 N/mm² to 12.09 N/mm² at 28 days for the highest replacement threshold, the resulting composite remains highly functional for lightweight and non-load-bearing civil engineering applications. These results prove that bamboo can be effectively used to replace up to 20–30% of coarse aggregates in concrete, helping reduce construction costs, supporting eco-friendly building practices, and mitigating the environmental impacts associated with natural stone quarrying1.
