AB  - On 6 August 2025 the United States imposed a 50 percent tariff on Brazilian goods and exempted a named list of lines. Customs did not collect 50 percent. The collected rate peaked at 22.3 percent of customs value in October; coffee was about 50 percent then and essentially zero in December, while quartzite stayed near 16.5 percent. Coffee and beef joined the annex on 13 November; quartzite and civil aircraft were exempt from day one. Using Census, Comex Stat and USITC records, the bilateral balance moved toward a U.S. surplus by $719 million a month (Census, after a trend and macroeconomic controls) and $1,346 million (Comex Stat, after a trend only) in September–October. Those are window associations, not a surplus created by the tariff. Non-exempt import values fell 66 percent relative to exempt products, concentrated in never-exempt stone and salt rather than later-exempt farm lines. Relative to quartzite, which was exempt throughout, dollar unit values of meat and coffee did not fall; they rose. A pooled diversion coefficient is indistinguishable from zero when coffee is coded as exempt-leaning. That average is a composition effect and is not robust to coding coffee as non-exempt: frozen beef, non-exempt throughout, is redirected to non-U.S. markets; orange juice, exempt throughout, is not. Chapter-level estimates can have the wrong sign when the instrument is an exemption schedule.
