AB  - The Indian Feldspar Mines in Fraser Township, Renfrew County, Ontario comprise a histori-cally worked group of granitic pegmatite occurrences centred on the Barr Feldspar Quarry (NorthMine) and the Collins workings (South Mine), principally associated with Lot 24, ConcessionXVI. The North occurrence was worked for feldspar by Walter J. Barr during the winters of1934-1936. Ontario records report 1,107 short tons of feldspar production from a probable 10,000tons of rock quarried, with the product shipped to Rochester, New York. Historical descriptionsrecord a curving, zoned pegmatite 30-40 ft wide, graphic-granite margins, a quartz-rich core,and microcline crystals reaching approximately 3 × 4 × 5 ft. Subsequent investigation identifiedlocalized uranium-bearing and rare-element mineralization including allanite, fergusonite, py-rochlore and uraninite; two selected samples submitted by E.R. Woermke in 1954 assayed 0.51%and 0.55% U3O8.This paper consolidates the known historical, geological, cadastral and field record andestablishes a modern research program. A brief 2026 reconnaissance by the author foundabundant historical quarry waste, metre-scale feldspar consistent with historical descriptions,large white and smoky quartz, coarse mica and numerous pits or trenches. These observationsdo not establish an economic mineral resource. They do, however, justify systematic mapping ofthe workings, host-rock contacts, waste piles and previously underexamined parts of the hill;modern characterization of Nb–Ta–Y–REE–U–Th-bearing accessory assemblages; industrial-mineral characterization of feldspar; and reconstruction of the site's human history. The project'sgoverning principle is characterization rather than confirmation: negative results, discontinuitiesand barren zones are to be recorded with the same weight as positive observations
