AB  - Recycling waste materials and reducing environmental pollution have become an engineering challenge. This study aims to investigate the performance of the incorporation of waste marble powder (MP) and lime-metakaolin (LC2) in sandy soil for deep soil mixing applications. In the first phase, soil was treated with cement (C) at 10, 20, and 30% contents and mixed at water-binder ratios of 0.8, 1, and 1.2. The results identified 30% cement content as the optimum dosage for further modification to ensure a better coating of soil particles and reduce heterogeneity that often occurs at lower dosages. In the second phase, the 30% cement dosage was taken as a reference binder (100%) and combined through the incorporation of different amounts of MP at C/MP ratios of 100/0, 95/5, 90/10, 85/15, and 80/20 at the same equivalent water-binder ratios. The third phase involved selecting a mixture of 85/15, C/MP, which represents the critical performance limits where strength declined while maximizing landfill diversion. LC2 was introduced to replace MP at various percentages in the OPC/MP/LC2 combinations of 85/13.5/1.5, 85/12.75/2.25, 85/10.5/4.5, and 85/7.5/7.5, along with the same water-binder ratios. To assess the engineering properties of the mixtures, bulk density, unconfined compressive strength, ultrasonic pulse velocity, scanning electron microscopy, and energy dispersive X-ray spectroscopy were conducted. The optimum MP and LC2 replacement percentages were found to be 10 and 2.25 %, respectively. Utilizing LC2 in 15% MP overcomes the unconfined compressive strength of the sand and optimum MP mixed with C and achieves good densification and homogeneity.
