AB  - Fiber-reinforced composites like Kevlar are widely used in engineering for their high strength-to-weight ratios. Ensuring their reliability requires Non-Destructive Testing (NDT) methods such as ultrasonic inspection, thermography, and acoustic emission. This work explores advancements in Kevlar-based composites, emphasizing sustainability and waste utilization. A major focus is on the use of Shear Thickening Fluids (STFs), which improve impact resistance up to 400% by optimizing silica content and padding pressure. Auxetic Kevlar laminates and multi-layered armor systems with ramie-epoxy layers offer enhanced toughness and cost savings of up to 95%. Silica-based STFs and 3D fabric structures further boost ballistic resistance and flexibility. A novel aspect of this study is the incorporation of waste marble dust into polymer matrices like epoxy, polypropylene, and unsaturated polyester. This sustainable additive improves flexural strength, impact resistance, and thermal stability while reducing material weight. The optimal marble dust content enhances compressive strength, hardness, and wear resistance, making these composites suitable for defense and protective gear applications. Advanced fabrication techniques such as Vacuum-Assisted Resin Transfer Molding, 3D printing, and additive manufacturing are also discussed for efficient production. These innovations contribute to cost-effective, high-performance materials with improved structural integrity and environmental benefits.
