AB  - Modern society faces socio-environmental challenges related to solid waste. In this context, sustainable and design and social innovation can play a significant role in addressing these issues. This study aimed to valorize two types of waste, sugarcane bagasse ash (CBC waste) and ornamental stone solid waste (RRO waste), by developing new soil-cement ceramic materials suitable for manufacturing soil-cement bricks. Cylindrical specimens containing upto 30 wt% CBC waste in replacement of Portland cement and cylindrical specimens up to 30 wt% RRO waste in replacement sandy soil were prepared by uniaxial pressing and cured for 28 days. The cured specimens were characterized in terms of water absorption and simple compression strength. The experimental results showed the feasibility of producing soil-cement bricks incorporating up to 30 wt% CBC waste and soil-cement bricks incorporanting up to 30 wt% RRO waste. These products are suitable for use in civil construction, particularly for social housing projects, thereby aligning with the principles of sustainable design and social innovation.
