AB  - This project explores the use of stone, pre-dimensioned stone blocks, as a load-bearing construction material in a contemporary context. Two mixed use blocks of flats with shops at street level are planned for central Oslo - mainly stone and wood, not concrete or steel, carry the loads. The building industry is currently confronted with an unprecedented challenge: the transition toward environmentally sustainable practices. About 39% of global carbon emissions are attributed to the construction and operation of buildings and urban infrastructure, with around 11% stemming from building materials alone. These emissions are largely the result of energy-intensive processes such as the firing of clay bricks, the smelting of steel, and the calcination of cement. Each of these burn large quantities of fuel. Quarried stone avoids most of those emissions. Stone has low or negligible embodied carbon when locally sourced, while also having very high compressive strength. It also has exceptional resistance to weather, fire, and water infiltration. It is inherently durable, low-maintenance, and, in principle, infinitely recyclable if assembled in a manner that allows for disassembly without structural damage. Stone construction follows a monomaterial logic in which structure, envelope, and surface coincide, resulting in a distinct architectural language compared to concrete and steel. Although modern load-bearing stone construction has seen a resurgence in parts of Europe, it remains rare in Northern Europe and effectively non-existent in Scandinavia, despite the widespread availability of standardized granite blocks in Norway. By basing the project on this material, the thesis brings together concerns of proportion, tectonic clarity, permanence, sustainability, and craft
