JO  - Australian Journal of Earth Sciences
DO  - 10.1080/08120099.2025.2463604
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1080/08120099.2025.2463604
AB  - Adelaide black granite is best recognised for its use as dimension stone in heritage buildings and monuments around Australia. This study investigates the geomechanical properties of Adelaide black granite to evaluate its potential suitability for use in geological disposal facilities and other underground applications, including CO2 and hydrogen storage and geothermal energy systems. Laboratory testing of near-surface samples sourced from the Black Hill Quarry assessed the intact physical and mechanical properties of the rock, including density, unconfined compressive strength, triaxial compressive strength, tensile strength, elastic modulus and Poisson ratio. Results indicate that intact samples of Adelaide black granite exhibit high density (3421 kg/m³) and exceptional strength and stiffness [average unconfined compressive strength (UCS): 246.4 MPa and elastic modulus: 72.6 GPa]. Comparisons with other granites used in global geological disposal facilities demonstrates that Adelaide black granite meets or exceeds international standards for host rock suitability. Empirically derived rock mass parameters, including the Geological Strength Index and Hoek–Brown constant mi, further support excavation stability in Adelaide black granite at depths up to 600 m. This depth compares favourably with other geological disposal facilities hosted in granite that are currently operating or under construction around the world. Although Adelaide black granite demonstrates favourable geomechanical characteristics, further investigation is recommended to confirm properties at greater depths not represented in this study. These findings establish Adelaide black granite as a promising candidate for advanced underground applications, contributing to Australia’s sustainable energy and waste management initiatives.
