TI  - Alkali-Silica Reactive Potential in Reactive Powder Concrete with Quartzite Tailings
AU  - Ivan Francklin
AU  - Rogério Pinto Ribeiro
AU  - Eduardo Goulart Collares
AU  - Eduardo Antonio de Castro Dutra
AU  - Ithalo Bruno Vilela Bueno
PY  - 2025
JO  - Revista de Gestão Social e Ambiental
DO  - 10.24857/rgsa.v19n9-090
UR  - https://doi.org/10.24857/rgsa.v19n9-090
AB  - Introduction: The municipality of São Thomé das Letras, in the southeastern region of Brazil, is an important quartzite-producing center. The extraction process generates approximately 90% waste based on the raw material extracted. To mitigate this impact, utilizing this waste in Reactive Powder Concrete (RPC) has emerged as a viable solution. However, studies on its durability are still scarce, especially on the possibility of alkali-silica reactivity. Thus, this study analyzed the potential for alkali-silica reactivity (ASR) in RPC bars produced with quartzite waste. The experimental approach included the characterization of the constituent materials, petrographic analysis of the quartzite, expansion test by the accelerated bar method, and microscopic analysis using Scanning Electron Microscopy (SEM), including active silica to assess its effects in one batche. The petrographic analysis revealed approximately 95% silica content on quartzite, where tests with the inclusion of active silica in RPC samples effectively mitigated the potential negative impacts of ASR. The average expansion index was low, and no typical reaction products were detected by SEM. However, peaks related to silico-alkaline compounds were recorded in the energy dispersive spectroscopy by EDX, therefore, ASR analysis should be conducted on RPC prisms using both accelerated and long-term methods. The diagnosis of ASR in quartzites corroborated the need to investigate the chemical reactions induced by ASR in special concretes made with these aggregates. In conclusion, based on the results obtained, quartzite tailings can be effectively used as an alternative aggregate in RPC, thereby reducing the amount of mining waste and minimizing negative environmental impacts. Objective: The objective of this study is to investigate the alkali-silica reactive potential in reactive powder concretes produced with the fine fraction of quartzite waste, and thereby verify the possibility of using this material as an alternative aggregate in special concretes. Theoretical Framework: Alkali-Silica Reaction (ASR) is a concrete pathology in which alkalis in cement react with silica in the aggregate. This reaction forms an expansive gel that causes structural deterioration. Mitigation involves limiting alkalis, reducing porosity, and using cements with additives. Reactive Powder Concrete (RPC) emerges as a sustainable alternative, using quartzite mining tailings as aggregate. It can also mitigate ASR with the use of silica fume and dosage studies that enable low porosity. Method: The methodology adopted for this research comprises producing two batches of RPC for each quartzite rejetct collected, one batch containing active silica and the other without. The research method included determining the chemical compositions of the cement to determine the alkali content, petrographic analysis of the quartzite samples, expansion testing using the accelerated method, and microstructure analysis using SEM. 
