AB  - This study presents the Outcrop Exposure Index (OEI) as a tool to support geological field planning in regolith-covered terrains with limited outcrop access. The OEI integrates the Lateritic Index (LI), derived from airborne gamma spectrometry, and slope data from digital elevation models. Through unsupervised classification, zones with similar weathering and geomorphological features were identified in three areas of interest (AOIs) in Bahia, Brazil. In the Maracás Sheet, the OEI supported geological and regolith mapping by highlighting areas to avoid or prioritize based on exposure conditions. In Serra de Jacobina and western Bahia, the OEI facilitated the identification of potential dimension stone outcrops, including black limestone bodies with calcite veining. The results demonstrate that OEI can effectively support fieldwork planning in areas with challenging surface conditions.
