AB  - A growing number of people are leading the construction industry away from pollution and resource use by making it greener and more sustainable. Consequently, green concrete is an environmentally friendly option that tries to cause less harm except in improvement of key characteristics. Fly ash from thermal plants, ground granulated blast furnace slag from steel sectors, silica fume from making silicon, rice husk ash from agriculture, red mud from making alumina and marble dust from processing stone can be used in green concrete as part or all of the cement and aggregates. They cut down on the amount of clinker-based cement needed and also improve some aspects of the product’s strength and durability thanks to their pozzolanic and filling actions. This review analyzes how these materials function in green concrete and reviews their influence on strength, workability, stiffness, protection from salt and sulfur, permeability, carbonation and durability in freeze-thaw conditions. The report highlights that environmental gain is one of the most significant benefits of green concrete, since it lowers greenhouse gas levels, helps manage waste wisely and uses less natural resources. The impact on profits from using industrial waste in large infrastructure projects is investigated. Although achievements have been impressive, uncertain waste characteristics, a lack of common mix designs, not much field data and inadequate information to stakeholders are obstacles to wider use. At the conclusion of the paper, possible future directions are highlighted such as developing improved characterization methods, nano-addition, detailed environmental models and suitable laws to manage huge implementation. All in all, this review shows that green concrete, if used correctly, helps support sustainable development of modern infrastructure in line with world-wide goals for conservation.
