AB  - Granite is widely used in the construction industry, and cutting or polishing granite produces very fine waste powder called granite slurry waste (GSW). This waste causes environmental problems and can affect people’s health when disposed of in open areas. Concrete is the most common building material, and using granite waste in concrete can reduce environmental issues such as CO₂ emissions and waste dumping. This study examines how concrete behaves when part of the cement is replaced with granite slurry waste. Different concrete mixes with water–cement ratios of 0.4 and 0.5 were tested for: • Water permeability • Abrasion resistance • Ultrasonic pulse velocity (UPV) • Modulus of elasticity The results showed that workability increased when granite waste was added. The depth of water penetration and modulus of elasticity improved with GSW. Maximum water penetration (85 mm) occurred at 30% GSW replacement. Abrasion loss increased slightly but stayed below 1 mm in all cases. Modulus of elasticity improved up to a 5% replacement. UPV results showed the concrete quality remained good. Overall, using GSW makes concrete cheaper, more eco-friendly, and helps reduce pollution.
