AU  - Hyeon-Il Eom
AU  - Gun-Cheol Lee
AU  - Sang‐Keun Oh
AU  - Young Hak Lee
AU  - Dae Jin Kim
AU  - Su-Min Kang
PY  - 2025
JO  - Journal of Building Engineering
DO  - 10.1016/j.jobe.2025.114606
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jobe.2025.114606
AB  - ABSTRACT Dimension stone claddings, widely used as nonstructural exterior finishes on apartment buildings, low-rise public facilities, and high-rise facades, are typically installed using dowel pin connections in accordance with construction specifications. However, the use of epoxy bonding has been increasingly observed in the field, often being applied sorely and in violation of standard guidelines. To assess the structural safety of epoxy bonding, this study conducted unit joint tests by varying key parameters: connection type, epoxy width, bonded length, and chemical treatment conditions. Test results revealed that the primary failure mode of epoxy joints was detachment at the interface between the epoxy and the angle surface. The maximum strength of the joints generally increased with the epoxy adhesion area, and the influence of epoxy width on strength was found to be more significant than that of the bonding length. Additionally, breakout tests under accelerated weathering conditions demonstrated that environmental degradation of epoxy durability had a substantial impact on joint performance. While epoxy-only connections exhibit limited reliability and durability, combining epoxy with dowel pins significantly enhances performance. These findings highlight the importance of proper application methods and durability considerations when epoxy bonding is used in stone cladding installations.
