AB  - Objective: Analyze the technical feasibility of reusing waste from the exploitation of three types of commercial granite (yellow granite, swallow gray and peacock green) as materials for applications in infrastructure works, including railway ballast, hydraulic macadam, coarse aggregate for concrete and pavement subgrade. Theoretical Framework: It is based on the principles of Circular Economy and Cleaner Production, highlighting the redirection of solid waste from the ornamental rock industry as inputs for new production cycles, with a focus on resource efficiency and mitigation of environmental liabilities. Method: The study combined field research with laboratory tests. Three companies in Espírito Santo were monitored, with operational data collected on volumes extracted and sold. The waste was characterized by physical-mechanical and chemical tests in accordance with ASTM, NBR and DNER Standards, evaluating parameters such as granulometry, grain shape, Los Angeles abrasion, apparent porosity, water absorption, simple compression strength and durability. Results and Discussion: The average waste generation rates ranged from 67.14% to 88.91%. The results showed that none of the granites met the technical criteria for use as railway ballast due to their grain shape and abrasion resistance. However, the gray and green granites demonstrated compatibility with the regulatory requirements for use as coarse aggregate in concrete, hydraulic macadam and pavement subgrade, provided they were subjected to appropriate crushing and granulometric selection processes. Research Implications: The research highlights the potential for valorizing commercial granite exploitation waste as a technically and environmentally viable alternative in road and civil infrastructure, contributing to the reduction of environmental impacts and final destination costs. Originality/Value: This is an unprecedented contribution by proposing and testing, based on empirical and normative data, reuse routes for Brazilian granite waste, expanding the possibilities of inserting these materials into the civil construction chain within the principles of sustainability and circular economy.
