TI  - Development of Low Cost Wollastonite-Based Negatively Charged Ceramic Microfiltration Membrane from Waste Marble Dust
AU  - Hemant Vaidhya
AU  - Pradip Kumar Tewari
AU  - Vikky Anand
PY  - 2025
JO  - ACS Sustainable Resource Management
DO  - 10.1021/acssusresmgt.5c00359
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1021/acssusresmgt.5c00359
AB  - Waste marble dust poses a significant environmental challenge as it is a non-biodegradable solid waste. Utilizing the waste marble dust to fabricate a cost-effective ceramic membrane not only provides a sustainable solution for waste management but also offers economic and environmental advantages. This study demonstrates the valorization of waste marble dust and natural clay as cost effective and sustainable raw materials to synthesize wollastonite phase and development of porous ceramic membranes, indicating an environmentally sustainable approach for membrane fabrication. The systematic investigation was conducted to evaluate the effect of sintering temperature (900 °C–1100 °C) on key properties, including porosity, mechanical strength, and chemical durability. The optimal performance was achieved at a sintering temperature of 1000 °C, with a composition having 40% wt marble dust, 20% wt % natural clay, and 20% wt fuller’s earth clay. The optimized ceramic membrane using waste marble dust was fabricated using a dry molding method. The membrane demonstrated an average pore radius of 0.32 μm and porosity of 40%. The mechanical strength of the membrane was found to be 14.11 MPa. Additionally, the hydraulic permeability of the membrane was 459 L/h m 2 bar, with excellent chemical stability in acidic and alkaline media. The overall fabrication cost of the membrane was estimated to be 75 USD/m 2, exceptionally lower than that of commercially available membranes. The membrane showed excellent mechanical and chemical properties, with a combined cost-effectiveness, presents a promising candidate for large-scale microfiltration application over mid to high temperature ranges. Overall, the study successfully demonstrates the valorization of marble dust as a promising alternative for sustainable membrane fabrication.
