AB  - In India, a sizable section of the land is covered in red earth. These earths are prevalent in regions with little precipitation, and they don't seem to be able to retain moisture. These types of earths need to be stabilized to enhance their shear strength and dilatancy. This research focuses on waste utilization in the process of earth stabilization. For this purpose, low-density polyethylene (LDPE) in the form of milk pouches and ceramic tile waste are employed as stabilizers in this investigation. An experimental program was undertaken to study the individuals and admixed effect of ceramic tile waste and plastic strips (LDPE) on the geotechnical characteristics of the collected red earth with varying ceramic tile dust and plastic content. Ceramic tile dust which passed through a 4.75mm IS sieve and LDPE plastic strips, 10cm long were randomly mixed with virgin earth. Ceramic tile dust was mixed with the earth sample by diverging proportions of 5%, 10%, 15%, 20%, 25%, and 30% of the dry wt. of the earth. Divergent tests, like standard penetration test (SPT), UCS test, CBR test, and direct shear test (DST) were carried out in this kind of earth. It was found that red earth with 25% tile dust gives the maximum CBR and UCS value. Furthermore, keeping 25% of marble dust constant, the earth was mixed with LDPE with varying proportions of 0.25%, 0.50%, 0.75%, 1.00%, and 1.25% of the dry wt. of the earth. A series of SPT, unsoaked CBR tests, and soaked CBR tests were conducted for these earth samples. The results showed that taking 25% tile dust with 0.75% LDPE gives the maximum values for maximum dry density (MDD), CBR, and UCS value. Based on the outcome, waste ceramic tile dust and waste LDPE plastic can be used, sooner or later, as very good additives to upgrade the geotechnical properties of red earth.
