AB  - Growing environmental concerns associated with Portland cement production have intensified the need to develop sustainable alternative construction materials. Metakaolin-based alkali-activated concretes have emerged as promising novel binders due to their significantly lower CO₂ emissions. In this study, the feasibility of using granite stone waste and porcelain ceramic waste in the production of metakaolin-based alkali-activated concrete was investigated. For this purpose, concrete specimens were prepared with different proportions of granite waste, porcelain ceramic waste, and limestone sand as replacements relative to metakaolin. Compressive strength and flexural strength tests were conducted at curing ages of 7 and 28 days, along with water absorption measurements and microstructural analysis using scanning electron microscopy (SEM). The results indicated that the incorporation of granite stone waste and porcelain ceramic waste led to compressive strength values of up to 46.1 MPa and flexural strength values of up to 4.43 MPa. Compared with limestone sand, these wastes increased compressive strength by up to 13.3% and flexural strength by up to 22.4%. However, the results also showed that the use of granite stone and porcelain ceramic wastes resulted in higher water absorption, ranging from 45.5% to 57.8%, compared with mixtures containing limestone sand. Microstructural investigations revealed no significant differences in terms of homogeneity and matrix continuity among the specimens. The primary reason for the observed differences in mechanical performance was attributed to the higher intrinsic strength of granite and porcelain ceramic wastes compared with limestone sand. Overall, the utilization of granite stone and porcelain ceramic wastes not only contributes to waste management and the reduction of environmental impacts, but also provides a suitable platform for the development of innovative, environmentally friendly concrete materials.
