AB  - As cities grow and industries expand quickly, more concrete is needed and this has made people worry more about the environment, especially the large amount of carbon dioxide released by cement and the disappearance of natural stones and gravel. It investigates how using industrial waste such as fly ash, ground granulated blast-furnace slag (GGBS), silica fume and recycled concrete aggregates (RCA) in cement and normal aggregates can influence green concrete’s development and productivity. Several mixes were formed from different ratios of the byproducts and their performance, in terms of strength and ease of working, was tested. Other aspects looked at for durability were sulfate attack, how much chloride can penetrate and the depth of carbonation, using standard tests. A life cycle assessment (LCA) was also performed to measure the environmental impact in terms of energy use and CO₂ emissions. It was proven that lighting new concrete mixtures show up to 7.5% greater strength at 28 days than ordinary concrete, with around a 35–50% drop in chloride penetration and a 40% better sulfate resistance. CO₂ emissions were reduced by as much as 40% and embodied energy by 30%, proving that the mixtures were good for the environment. This research creates a framework that can help mix industrial waste into concrete and thus reduces both resource use and harm to the environment in current construction projects.
