TY  - THES
TI  - Grão crematório-parque: anteprojeto com ênfase nas atmosferas arquitetônicas
AU  - Oliveira, Fernanda Lorena Rabelo de
PY  - 2025
JO  - UFRN
UR  - https://repositorio.ufrn.br/handle/123456789/68133
AB  - This Professional Master’s Degree Final Project aims to conceive a crematorium-park with an emphasis on potential architectural atmospheres. In the current context, crematoria present themselves as an alternative to address environmental and land-use issues associated with conventional cemeteries, while also offering greater flexibility in the face of cultural transformations related to death, dying, and mourning. The study was based on reflections on death in contemporary society and on the evolution of cremation practices, with a focus on the Brazilian context. It also discussed the foundations of phenomenology and its influence on architecture, in order to understand how this philosophical approach, whose existential intersections directly engage with the theme, can guide the design of funerary spaces. The analysis of reference projects, in turn, expanded the repertoire and provided a more empirical understanding. Added to this was the immersion in the intervention site, located in the neighborhood of Felipe Camarão, in Natal/RN, aimed at identifying physical, environmental, and legal constraints. Based on this process, design dilemmas and goals were established with the intention of overcoming a strictly functional perspective, incorporating symbolic dimensions capable of engaging with diverse audiences. The design concept sought to balance material and immaterial aspects, reconciling the diversity of uses with the surroundings and respecting the ritual-architectural path of the bereaved. Thus, Grão, as it was named, presents in its final version the integration between natural and built environments, structured through the unfolding of eight sectors across the site: (1) Psychology of Mourning, (2) Reception, (3) Administration, (4) Cremation, (5) Ceremony, (6) Memory, (7) Support, and (8) Living, totaling 5,876.73 m² of built area. The site plan seeks to enhance the permeability of the park, which envelops and connects the buildings, making the landscape a structuring element of the experience. From a technical standpoint, the preliminary design adopts feasible structural solutions, associated with materials of strong symbolic and sensory value, such as terracotta toned cyclopean concrete, natural stone, and wood. Sustainable strategies include rainwater harvesting, green roofs, cross ventilation, natural lighting, and the use of native species in landscaping, fostering biodiversity and local identity. As a result, the population of Rio Grande do Norte is offered the proposal of a funerary facility integrated into a park, compatible with emerging demand and capable of encompassing all stages of the rite – from separation to transition and the incorporation of memory.
