AB  - Abstract The reuse of granite and marble waste as inert pigments in eco-friendly paints shows great potential. However, the absence of effective dispersion and stabilization processes, combined with the low biological resistance of these formulations, may compromise their performance. This study investigated the effects of biocidal and dispersant additives on the performance of PVA latex paints made from granite and marble waste. The results indicated that the ideal dosages corresponded to up to 1.0% biocide and below 0.02 dispersant/pigment ratio, ensuring adequate stability, bacterial resistance, and mechanical performance. The biocide reduced bacterial growth from 10⁶ to 10¹ CFU/mL. In contrast, while dispersant improved pigment dispersion and increased hiding power, it reduced abrasion resistance by over 70%. In summary, this study provides guidance for formulating sustainable paints with optimized performance, recommending low dispersant and adequate biocide contents.
