PY  - 2025
JO  - Procedia Computer Science
DO  - 10.1016/j.procs.2025.01.086
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.procs.2025.01.086
AB  - This research explores the dynamics of coopetition networks among small and medium enterprises (SMEs), particularly highlighting the role of technological innovation through the Industrial Internet of Things (IIoT) in enhancing the sustainability and resilience of these networks. Despite extensive studies on SME coopetition networks, a significant research gap exists regarding how IIoT can specifically bolster these networks. This study aims to address this gap by investigating how this technology can facilitate coopetition among SMEs, anchored in the Service-Dominant Logic (S-D Logic) framework that emphasizes a service-centric value cocreation perspective. The research employs a Service Design approach to conduct an in-depth analysis of nine ornamental stone SMEs, examining various stages of customer interaction and documenting the impact of different market approach strategies on business outcomes. Utilizing the Service Blueprint tool, the study identifies a clear divergence in outcomes based on these strategies. Independent companies often fail to close deals, highlighting a critical challenge in achieving market success. Conversely, firms collaborating with rivals secured deals but encountered post-sale disputes, negatively impacting customer satisfaction. Significantly, companies integrated into a coopetition network supported by an IIoT-based system successfully closed deals and maintained harmonious relations with rivals and customers. It illustrates the capacity of IIoT technology to enable effective coopetition in SME networks. Despite its focus on a specific sector and a limited sample size, this study lays the foundation for future research on the scalability of technology-enabled coopetition models and their long-term impacts on SME performance and customer relationships.
