TI  - Initiating Industry 4.0 in a traditional sector: Strategic digital transformation at a SME
AU  - Elisa Nezamoutdinova de Jesus Almeida
PY  - 2025
JO  - Repositório do ISCTE-IUL
UR  - http://hdl.handle.net/10071/36015
AB  - Industry 4.0 represents a paradigm shift in manufacturing, integrating digital technologies such as IoT, cyber-physical systems, and smart automation to enhance efficiency, real-time planning, and sustainability. Its adoption, however, requires not only technological upgrades but also cultural and strategic transformation. This dissertation evaluated the Industry 4.0 readiness of Mármores Rosal, a SME in the Portuguese ornamental stone sector, and developed a strategic roadmap to guide its digital transition. A mixed-methods approach was applied, combining qualitative and quantitative techniques. The qualitative phase involved a literature review, sectoral analysis, and strategic frameworks (SWOT, PESTEL, Porter’s Five Forces), complemented by semi-structured interviews, observations, and document analysis to capture cultural readiness and internal dynamics. The quantitative phase applied the Schumacher Industry 4.0 Maturity Model (2016) across eight dimensions, with data collected through an inquiry. The results showed an overall maturity level of around 3 out of 5, indicating a developing stage. “Leadership” and “Strategy” scored above average, reflecting managerial commitment to innovation, while “Technology” and “Products” scored lower due to weak IT infrastructure and limited digital integration. Cultural barriers, workforce constraints, and scarce external partnerships were also identified. Enablers included strong leadership, an established Quality Management System, and available public funding for digitalization. Based on these findings, a roadmap was proposed with phased actions addressing culture, people, governance, and technology, combining short-term pilot projects with longer-term strategic initiatives. The study contributes to the literature by applying a maturity model in a traditional SME context and offers practical guidance for the ornamental stone sector as it navigates Industry 4.0 transformation.
