AU  - Zheng Li
PY  - 2025
JO  - Results in Engineering
DO  - 10.1016/j.rineng.2025.108775
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.rineng.2025.108775
AB  - The innovative material modification strategy provides a dual solution to longstanding challenges in construction materials engineering, effectively addressing both the intrinsic rigidity constraints of artificial stone and the environmental burdens associated with solid waste accumulation. By integrating styrene-acrylic emulsion (SAE) into a multi-component cementitious matrix comprising circulating fluidized bed fly ash (CFBFA), carbide slag (CS), and flue gas desulfurization gypsum (FGDG), we developed a synergistic approach to enhance mechanical flexibility while maintaining structural integrity. Through systematic comparison of compressive and bending strength parameters across experimental groups, coupled with advanced characterization techniques (SEM/CT), the modification mechanism was elucidated. Optimal performance was achieved at 4 % SAE dosage under 70 °C hot-pressing conditions, yielding concurrent improvements in bending strength (27.27 % increase) and compressive strength (12.42 % enhancement). Microstructural analysis revealed that SAE facilitates three-dimensional network formation through polymer-solid waste-based cementitious materials co-curing, effectively bridging microcracks and densifying pore structures under thermo-compressive activation. The incorporation of SAE induced a remarkable 51.06 % reduction in porosity within the composite system, demonstrating significant microstructural densification. Freeze-thaw tests (25 cycles) conducted on the optimal 4 % SAE composite revealed an upward trend in mass and compressive strength loss rates with increasing cycles, yet the material remained compliant with Chinese National Standard GB/T 50082–2024, thereby validating its superior durability. And the study proposes an innovative strategy for performance enhancement in artificial stone fabrication, offering new perspectives for mechanical property optimization in solid waste-based construction materials.
