AB  - With the growth of cities, their resilience becomes more critical for the safety and durability of buildings. Amman is a city having the natural stone façades for most of its residential buildings. This paper studies strategies for resilient residential buildings through regular maintenance requirements and reduction of the risks associated with façade deterioration. This has been achieved by inspecting 40 local residential building façades of different ages, classified into 4 groups, over 30 years and implementing simple experimental methods for detecting the quality of the material used in the façades of certain anomalies that affect the resilience of the building. A cumulative probability approach was used to predict the occurrence of anomalies over time. It is found that most anomalies have a very high likelihood of occurrence over 50 years, especially aesthetic anomalies, mortar removal, and fissures. Accordingly, this study highlights the urgent requirement to address building façade flaws to maintain public safety and raises awareness of the potential hazards of neglect and promotes maintenance as a key factor in long-term façade management. It is imperative to establish quality standards and protocols in the construction industry to avoid severe catastrophic failures and confirm the long-term sustainability of building façades.
