AU  - K. Afshar
PY  - 2025
JO  - American Journal of Respiratory and Critical Care Medicine
DO  - 10.1164/ajrccm.2025.211.abstracts.a1858
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1164/ajrccm.2025.211.abstracts.a1858
AB  - Abstract Rationale: Silicosis, a lung disease caused by silica dust inhalation, has resurged among engineered countertop workers in Southern California, primarily affecting Hispanic immigrants. Silicosis associated with artificial stone masonry is more aggressive than that from natural stone masonry due to higher silica content. As a result, lung transplants for silicosis due to engineered stone have increased, yet transplant outcomes for this population remain understudied. This study defines one-year post-lung transplant outcomes for patients with silicosis at UC San Diego Health and analyzes factors associated with these outcomes compared to patients transplanted for non-silicosis interstitial lung disease (ILD). Methods: Using the electronic health record and United Network for Organ Sharing database, we identified patients with silicosis and non-silicosis ILD who underwent lung transplant at UC San Diego Health from 2019 to 2023. Data on patient demographics and history was collected and analyzed via descriptive statistics, logistic regression models, students t-test, and one-way ANOVA. Results: Of the 85 patients identified, 7 (7.6%) had been diagnosed with silicosis and 85 (92.4%) with non-silicosis ILDs. The mean age of the silicosis patients was 43 years (SD = 10; non-silicosis ILD: M = 60, SD = 7; p < 0.001). All silicosis patients were male (non-silicosis ILD: male = 51, female = 34) and 6 (85.7%) were Hispanic/Latino (non-silicosis ILD: 35 (41.2%)). Prior to lung transplant, the mean FEV1 and FVC of silicosis patients was 24% (SD = 15%; non-silicosis ILD: M = 46%, SD = 11%; p < 0.001) and 28% (SD = 10%; non-silicosis ILD: M = 43%, SD = 10%; p < 0.01). At one year, patients with silicosis had no differences in survival (100.0% vs 84.7%; p > 0.05), allograft rejection rates (silicosis = 14.3%; non-silicosis ILD = 23.4%; p > 0.05), rates of leukopenia (silicosis = 71.4%; non-silicosis ILD = 70.7%; p > 0.05), and FEV1 (silicosis = 75%; non-silicosis ILD = 81%; p > 0.05) and FVC (silicosis = 74%; non-silicosis ILD = 82%; p > 0.05). Conclusion: Prior to lung transplant, patients with silicosis presented younger and with more severe lung disease compared to patients with non-silicosis ILD. However, there was no difference in survival, rates of rejection, or pulmonary function tests at one-year compared to patients with non-silicosis ILD. Therefore, lung transplantation should be considered for patients with end-stage silicosis due to artificial stone despite increased disease severity at time of transplantation.
