AB  - Some of the content related to Earth sciences (rocks and minerals) appears explicitly inthe Primary Education curriculum, while the rest is found in areas other than experimentalsciences. This can hinder students’ acquisition of Earth science knowledge due toteachers’ erroneous decontextualization. Furthermore, preservice teachers demonstratelittle interest in learning Earth science content, hindering the development of adequateprofessional competence. The aim of this paper is to design a teaching sequence thatenables preservice teachers to acquire knowledge and skills that ensure effectivelearning of Earth Science content in the classroom. The teaching sequence consists of apractical case study on ornamental rock in a holistic context that involves multiple areasof knowledge (social sciences, technology, economics, etc.). The sequence exploresthe concept of ornamental rock, its characteristics, application, and social impact of thenatural stone sector, and its structure enables its transformation into a learning situationaccording to the LOMLOE curriculum for Primary Education. It is designed with a STEMperspective within a TPACK framework, promoting the visibility of Earth Science byaddressing content in a real-life context that is familiar to students
