AB  - Abstract Geopolymer paver blocks constitute a promising advancement in sustainable construction industry, leveraging the industrial by-products for developing ideal alternates to the traditional concrete. This innovative approach not only improves mechanical properties and reduces curing time but also considerably lowers the carbon emissions related to conventional cement manufacturing units. This review article is an attempt to delve into the development and performance of geopolymer paver blocks as an ecological substitute to conventional concrete, highlighting the usage of industrial by-products such as fly ash, ground granulated blast furnace slag, granite waste and red mud, which improve mechanical strength, durability and workability when optimized well, also developing flexural strength and fatigue resistance. Moreover, the usage of earthen materials such as clay and soil promotes the mitigation of carbon footprint concurrently improving the thermal properties making it an energy efficient construction. Life cycle assessment studies also indicated that geopolymer blocks have a lower CO 2 footprint than conventional concrete, regardless of the concerns of sodium silicate production. Also, thorough testing and precise assessment of replacement ratios, the long-term performance of these sustainable materials makes geopolymer paver blocks a viable solution for the demands in the modern infrastructure practices.
