AB  - According to the World Health Organisation, the total number of international migrants has almost doubled over the past three decades, estimated at 281 million in 2020 (https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789240054462), with the majority being economic migrants. Further increases are expected as a consequence of growing labour demand in an aging Western population, ongoing multiple geopolitical conflicts and the consequences of climate change. Migrant workers are an extremely vulnerable group. They often have limited access to medical care, particularly in those countries where healthcare access is restricted by insurance schemes or other financial constraints. When they do come in contact with health care professionals, language barriers and other cultural aspects influencing communication can have a major negative impact leading to miscommunication and delays in diagnosis and management plans. Importantly, migrant workers are particularly vulnerable to exploitation and health hazards in the workplace. They are often employed to work in the jobs not taken up by the natives, so called 3D jobs, for example, dangerous, dirty, demanding/degrading. The uncertainty of employment coupled with urgent financial need result in absence of bargaining power. This together with lack of information, safety training and protective measures mean that migrant workers are much more likely than the general population to be exposed to hazardous jobs in poor working conditions, leading to the onset of work-related pathology, including respiratory diseases. (A) Axial CT section (soft-tissue windows) showing complicated silicosis with progressive massive fibrosis. There are bilateral perihilar conglomerate masses with punctate calcification. Mediastinal calcified lymph nodes are also demonstrated. (B) Lung window settings at the same level show coexisting nodularity and emphysema. Reproduced from J. Feary, A. Devaraj, M. Burton, et al., “Artificial Stone Silicosis: A UK Case Series,” Thorax 79, (2024): 979–981 with permission from BMJ Publishing Group Ltd. In Australia, the rapid rise in cases of accelerated fatal silicosis in young individuals has led to a total ban on the use of AS in 2024. However, AS continues to be used in the rest of the world. There is clearly an urgent need for tight regulation, harsh penalties for companies not using adequate protective measures, regular screening to allow early detection and worker protection. The high mortality and morbidity of the disease and the difficulties in guaranteeing employers' and workers' awareness of the risks associated with artificial stone, with implementation of adequate protective measures in all work environments would be arguments in favour of a total ban on AS. Ultimately, in a democracy, we should be able to implement and enforce safe working conditions for migrants and all workers. Close attention towards the most vulnerable members of our society will lead to better working conditions for all. The
