AB  - Koksharovsky Hill is the most famous cult site in the Middle Transurals. Over the years of research, the collection of stone items has exceeded 31.5 thousand pieces. While ceramics and objects of a sacred nature are well represented in many publications, stone inventory is described piecemeal and selectively. The purpose of the article is to introduce the 2012 excavations stone inventory of the hill from the into scientific circulation. Processing of the stone items in focus im-plies statistical-typological, technological and tracological research methods. The study also determines the mineralogical composition of the stone complex. The collection includes stone items from three eras: Mesolithic (7.9%), Neolithic (91.3%) and Eneolithic (0.8%). The microlitic complex in all basic parameters (splitting technique, plate width, found cutters and covers) fully corresponds to the characteristics of the Middle Transurals Mesolithic. In the Neolithic era, the technique of stone splitting aims to obtain wider plates. All the main types of tools – knives, arrowheads, points, scrapers, drills – were made from plates. Un-retouched plates are mainly waste products as they are crooked, curved or have irregular facets. Almost half (47%) of the unretouched plates are cut-off distal and proximal tips. The plates exposed to secondary processing make up 58.2% of the collection. Moreover, 81% of the retouched plates are retouched from the back. The plates were preferably made from weakly siliceous rocks: light gray weakly siliceous rock and siltstone (79%). The tool complex is absolutely domi-nated by scrapers – there are 142 items. Knives and tiles were mainly manufac-tured using siliceous and carbonaceous slate. Polished tools list two knives and a large number of fragments of chopping tools (128 items), mainly adzes. All the polished tools are made of greenish volcanomictic sandstone. The grinding plates (126 items) are represented exclusively by fragments. They are made of various shales and granodiorite (biotitic granite). The collection contains 145 talc chunks of, 18 talc patches and 10 blanks of incomprehensible items. Among other pieces, it is necessary to note fishing sinkers, pestles and chisel-shaped tools. Notable complexes on the hill include blanks of tools (25 items) and flakes with retouched edges (139 items). Flakes (1,150 items), fragments, tiles, shapeless pieces, whole and split pebbles complete the stone collection. The mineral raw materials list 18 varieties. The raw materials were local; about 40% of the products have a prima-ry crust on their surface. The Eneolithic complex is defined on the basis of ar-rowheads, plate processing techniques and mineral raw materials atypical for the local Neolithic. Кокшаровский холм является самым известным культовым памятником на территории Среднего Зауралья. За годы исследований коллекция каменных изделий превысила 31,5 тысяч экз. И если керамика и предметы сакрального характера хорошо представлены во мно
