AB  - Abstract:The growing demand for natural sand in concrete production creates major environmental and economic challenges, while the marble industry generates large quantities of waste that pollute soil and water resources. This study investigates the use of waste marble powder (WMP) as a sustainable replacement for natural fine aggregate in structural concrete. Three mixes were prepared with 0% (MD0), 50% (MD50), and 100% (MD100) replacement of sand. Cube specimens were tested for compressive strength at 7, 14, 21, and 30 days. Durability indicators—including sorptivity, porosity, hardened unit weight, ultrasonic pulse velocity (UPV), and dynamic modulus of elasticity—were also measured at 30 days. The results showed that replacing natural sand with WMP did not reduce compressive strength. Mixtures with 50% and 100% replacement achieved slightly higher 30-day strengths compared to the control, due to the filler effect of fine marble particles. Durability properties improved significantly: sorptivity and porosity decreased by up to ~12% and ~11%, respectively, while UPV and dynamic modulus increased, indicating a denser and stiffer microstructure. These findings confirm that WMP can fully replace natural fine aggregate without compromising structural performance, contributing to sustainable construction and reducing environmental impact from marble waste. Keywords: waste marble powder, concrete, fine aggregate replacement, compressive strength, durability, UPV, sustainability
