TI  - Under-diagnosis of Silicosis by Pathologists in Current Global Epidemic of Accelerated Silicosis in Engineered Stone Workers
AU  - Jerrold L. Abraham
PY  - 2025
JO  - American Journal of Respiratory and Critical Care Medicine
DO  - 10.1164/ajrccm.2025.211.abstracts.a4210
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1164/ajrccm.2025.211.abstracts.a4210
AB  - Abstract Rationale: The ongoing global epidemic of severe silicosis in engineered stone workers often presents as accelerated silicosis, developing in less than 10 years latency. When biopsy, lung transplantation, or autopsy tissues are examined by pathologists, the early lesions of accelerated silicosis may not be recognized. Methods: trans-bronchial, explant and autopsy lung tissues from men with silicosis from manufacture of engineered stone are examined using routine brightfield and polarized light microscopy (PLM) and reveal a variety of histologic lesions. Results: Lesions observed range from obvious Progressive Massive Fibrosis, classic or developing silicotic nodules, diffuse interstitial disease and mixed dust alveolar proteinosis to nearly normal lung. The earlier cellular interstitial lesions, especially in trans-bronchial biopsies, have been unrecognized and unreported or mis-diagnosed as some non-dust-induced interstitial inflammatory disease. If pathologists do not routinely look for tiny particles nor look with adequate PLM for birefringent particles the evidence of such exposure will not be seen or reported. Most pathologists are unfamiliar with this least-recognized form of silicosis, especially in the absence of classic silicotic nodules. Furthermore, if diagnoses are done using current digital whole slide imaging (WSI), the resolution may be inadequate to visualize the many submicrometer size particles, and no option for PLM imaging exists with current diagnostic WSI. The Figure (from explanted lung of an engineered stone worker) shows an example of an early peribronchiolar/interstitial lesion of accelerated silicosis in which the dust-related nature of the interstitial disease process would be easily overlooked without high resolution brightfield and PLM imaging. Such lesions are commonly present in explanted and autopsy lungs and may be the only diagnostic lesions seen in limited sampling with transbronchial biopsies. Conclusions: Early macular, nodular and diffuse cellular lesions in accelerated silicosis and other dust-related diseases may be overlooked and mis-diagnosed by pathologists if no PLM is used, and/or when using only digital WSI. Additional training and continuing education of pathologists is needed to ensure correct and early diagnosis of accelerated silicosis. This is essential to eliminate further exposure, enable treatment, and support potential compensation.
